# rules: bad-hexa-conversion
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-704.kt
# from: kotlin/lang/security/bad-hexa-conversion.yaml
#
# `Integer.toHexString` drops the leading zero of a byte below 0x10, so a
# digest rendered by calling it once per byte comes out shorter than it should
# and is not a one-to-one encoding of the bytes any more. Two different digests
# can therefore render to the same string, which is a collision introduced by
# the formatting rather than by the hash - and a comparison of those strings is
# what a signature check usually comes down to.
#
# As translated the rule asked for the digest and the conversion to be
# statements in the same function, with the digest bound by
# `val $MD: MessageDigest = ...` - the typed spelling, which is the one Kotlin
# programs least often write. It matched nothing.
#
# What is reported now is the conversion, in a file that computes a digest at
# all. That is the line to change, and the file is the right unit because the
# hex helper is nearly always a separate function from the digest it renders -
# which is exactly why the original's same-function assumption could not hold.
#
# `%02x` and `toString(16).padStart(2, '0')` are the two spellings that keep
# the zero, and neither is this.

["Integer.toHexString($X)", "$I.toHexString($X)",
 "java.lang.Integer.toHexString($X)"] as $renders
| ["$MD.digest($$$_)", "$MD.getInstance($$$_)", "MessageDigest.getInstance($$$_)",
   "$M.doFinal($$$_)"] as $digests
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $renders + $digests) as $all
| ($all | of($renders) | in_files_with($all | of($digests)))
| finding("bad-hexa-conversion";
    "toHexString drops the leading zero of a byte below 0x10, so two different "
    + "digests can render to the same string; \"%02x\" instead")
| report
